docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:119:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01WK2Mq6zkdLg6Euiw8pQywv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011nh2BJCzpeGeBHs3Fy9gWy","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs","offset":260,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10676,"cache_read_input_tokens":37220,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10676,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbdQyD8DbWiNRxRFHe822D","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"30481d55-4fc2-4dfe-9854-62009674a87e","request_id":"req_011Cb7Lceh6oK4rnwXwRREE7","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore extension + MCP surface"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:123:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EtHZWdfsGtDouDbkw1GJHM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Structured Memory\n2\t\n3\t## Summary\n4\t\n5\tPhase 5 introduces a structured assertion layer on top of the existing durable memory store. The goal is not to replace freeform memory content, but to persist enough verification, provenance, freshness, and relationship metadata that later assistant workflows can reuse memory with an explainable trust order.\n6\t\n7\tThe current implementation is split between:\n8\t\n9\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/model.rs` for the structured-memory types\n10\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` for SQLite persistence, additive migration, default derivation, and recall ordering\n11\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/agent.rs` for assistant-facing ranking, highlight generation, and compact/full memory bundle shaping\n12\t\n13\tThis note describes only behavior verified in those areas. It does not define a new daemon or MCP contract.\n14\t\n15\t## Why This Exists\n16\t\n17\tBefore Phase 5, memory rows were durable and searchable, but most trust judgment still had to be inferred from freeform text, confidence, and recency. That makes reuse fragile in longer coding sessions:\n18\t\n19\t- a newer note can be weaker than an older verified one\n20\t- an observation can be stale after file or symbol changes\n21\t- an old memory can be contradicted or superseded without assistants seeing that clearly\n22\t- workflow outputs can reuse memory without telling the assistant why a memory should be trusted\n23\t\n24\tThe structured layer makes those states explicit so memory reuse can be both safer and easier to explain.\n25\t\n26\t## Data Model\n27\t\n28\tEach memory row still keeps the original core fields such as `content`, `memory_type`, `scope`, `confidence`, `linked_symbols`, `linked_files`, `workspace_id`, `branch`, `refresh_key`, and stale flags.\n29\t\n30\tPhase 5 adds a parallel structured assertion model:\n31\t\n32\t- `assertion_type`\n33\t- `verification_status`\n34\t- `confidence_reason`\n35\t- `supersedes_memory_id`\n36\t- `superseded_by_memory_id`\n37\t- `contradicts_memory_ids`\n38\t- `contradicted_by_memory_ids`\n39\t- `freshness_policy`\n40\t- `freshness_policy_detail`\n41\t- `provenance`\n42\t- `evidence`\n43\t\n44\tThe assertion vocabulary is wider than the legacy `memory_type`. In addition to observation, decision, exploration, pattern, and anti-pattern, the structured layer also supports `workflow_outcome` and `constraint`.\n45\t\n46\tThe verification lifecycle currently supports:\n47\t\n48\t- `unverified`\n49\t- `in_review`\n50\t- `verified`\n51\t- `stale`\n52\t- `contradicted`\n53\t- `superseded`\n54\t\n55\tFreshness is also modeled explicitly through `session_scoped`, `branch_scoped`, `repo_scoped`, `time_bound`, and `manual_review`.\n56\t\n57\t## Persistence And Migration\n58\t\n59\tStructured memory is stored in the existing `memories` SQLite table. The schema is extended additively with new scalar columns and JSON columns for contradiction edges, provenance, and evidence.\n60\t\n61\tThe migration approach is intentionally low-risk:\n62\t\n63\t- `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` defines the full current schema for fresh databases\n64\t- older databases are upgraded with additive `ALTER TABLE` statements for each structured column\n65\t- new indexes are added for `verification_status` and `superseded_by_memory_id`\n66\t- the FTS table is rebuilt after initialization so legacy rows remain searchable\n67\t\n68\tThis means old databases continue to open without a destructive rewrite. Legacy rows receive column defaults at the database level. Structured defaults are then derived on later store/update paths when Lattice rewrites a memory row.\n69\t\n70\t## Default Structured Derivation\n71\t\n72\t`MemoryStore::store` resolves structured metadata before persisting a row.\n73\t\n74\tFor a row that already has structured fields, the store preserves them and reconciles a few fields with current row state:\n75\t\n76\t- extended `assertion_type` values such as `workflow_outcome` and `constraint` are preserved instead of being reset from the legacy `memory_type`\n77\t- legacy-compatible assertion types continue to follow `memory_type`\n78\t- scope-derived freshness policies are recomputed from the current scope\n79\t- `stale` is forced if `memory.is_stale` is true\n80\t- a previously stale row falls back to inferred verification when it is no longer stale\n81\t- default provenance and evidence are filled in if missing\n82\t\n83\tFor a row with no structured metadata yet, the store derives defaults from the existing row:\n84\t\n85\t- `assertion_type` comes from `memory_type`\n86\t- `verification_status` is inferred from row state\n87\t- `freshness_policy` comes from scope\n88\t- `confidence_reason` is synthesized from a verification-like `source_query` or low confidence\n89\t- provenance is seeded from `source_query`, `refresh_key`, or an `assistant_observation` fallback\n90\t- evidence is seeded from linked symbols and linked files\n91\t\n92\tThe current verification inference is conservative:\n93\t\n94\t- stale rows become `stale`\n95\t- rows whose `source_query` contains verification signals such as `verified`, `validated`, `from code`, `from tests`, or `code and tests` become `verified`\n96\t- otherwise rows at `confidence >= 0.95` become `in_review`\n97\t- everything else remains `unverified`\n98\t\n99\t## Supersession, Contradiction, And Staleness Semantics\n100\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"0a8a689b-7c0e-47a7-b99d-5f3320391a8b","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.700Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:126:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PZbyg4u7BcDq7HcUUxNiXm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Stable Follow-Up Handles\n2\t\n3\t## Summary\n4\t\n5\tPhase 1 of the assistant usefulness roadmap now uses stable follow-up expansion where the graph identity is known. Symbol-name targets are still supported, but they are no longer the preferred contract for assistant-facing follow-up when a node can be identified exactly.\n6\t\n7\tThe graph model already has a durable identity basis: `SymbolId` is keyed by `file`, `name`, and `byte_offset`. The Phase 1 contract is to reuse that durable identity where possible and to carry a stable handle through workflow output, suggested follow-up targets, cached seeds, and `expand_context` resolution.\n8\t\n9\t## Why This Exists\n10\t\n11\tCurrent assistant-facing follow-up targets often look like `symbol:<name>`. That is usable when names are unique, but it breaks down in real codebases:\n12\t\n13\t- two files can export the same helper name\n14\t- a subsystem can contain the same function name in a primary file and a support file\n15\t- a query can surface both a pivot and a supporting context node with the same symbol label\n16\t\n17\tIn those cases, a name-only target forces `expand_context` to guess. That guess is acceptable as a fallback, but it is not a durable contract for assistant-driven code work.\n18\t\n19\t## Durable Identity Basis\n20\t\n21\tThe repository already has the stable identity needed for Phase 1:\n22\t\n23\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/symbols.rs` defines `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }`\n24\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/model.rs` stores that identifier on every `GraphNode`\n25\t\n26\tFor this phase, the handle should be derived from that identity instead of only from the symbol label. The exact encoding is an implementation detail, but the handle must be deterministic for the same graph node and must distinguish duplicate names across files and offsets.\n27\t\n28\t## Contract\n29\t\n30\tPhase 1 follows this resolution order:\n31\t\n32\t1. Resolve an exact stable handle if one is present.\n33\t2. If no exact handle is available, resolve a file-qualified or symbol-qualified target using the current backward-compatible behavior.\n34\t3. Preserve fuzzy name matching only as a fallback path for older clients and older cached payloads.\n35\t\n36\tThat means:\n37\t\n38\t- exact handle lookup is the preferred path\n39\t- the old `symbol:<name>` shape must continue to work during migration\n40\t- `expand_context` should return the intended node when a stable handle is available, even if a duplicate-name node exists elsewhere in the graph\n41\t\n42\t## Assistant-Facing Surfaces\n43\t\n44\tThe implemented assistant-facing surfaces that emit stable handles or preserve them in cached seeds are:\n45\t\n46\t- `get_context_capsule`\n47\t- `prepare_change`\n48\t- `impact_from_diff`\n49\t- `get_working_set_context`\n50\t- `summarize_subsystem`\n51\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n52\t- `diagnose_failure`\n53\t- helper paths that currently synthesize `suggested_expand`\n54\t\n55\tThe Phase 1 goal is not to remove the existing `file:` and `symbol:` surfaces immediately. It is to make them carry stable identity where available, and to make `expand_context` prefer that identity first.\n56\t\n57\t## Current Implementation Touchpoints\n58\t\n59\tThe stable-handle implementation now lives in these areas:\n60\t\n61\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/agent.rs`\n62\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`\n63\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/symbols.rs`\n64\t\n65\tThose paths now emit stable handles where the graph node identity is known and keep legacy forms available as fallback.\n66\t\n67\t## Migration Notes\n68\t\n69\tThis change stays backward-compatible for cached handles and older clients:\n70\t\n71\t- keep accepting existing handle payloads\n72\t- keep accepting legacy `symbol:<name>` follow-up targets\n73\t- prefer stable handles in compact follow-up suggestions and cached seeds when the node can be resolved exactly\n74\t\n75\tThe important boundary is behavioral, not cosmetic: after this phase, follow-up expansion should be deterministic enough that assistants can use it for code actions without guessing which duplicate symbol was intended.\n76\t\n77\t## Verification Status\n78\t\n79\tManual consistency pass only.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"9657e096-bcbf-4260-9740-eeba7b96c8f7","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.867Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
